const governance = authored({
  id:'databricks-unity-catalog-access',title:'SELECT Is Not the Whole Permission to Read a Table',topic:'Making feature access explicit',
  blurb:"The table is there, and a colleague can read it. Why does the scheduled training job still fail to access the same records?",openingTitle:'The Model Job Can See the Table Name but Cannot Read Its Rows',
  opening:['Botie’s experiment manifest names the right table. The scheduled job fails when it tries to read it, even though someone granted SELECT. The person who explored the data in a notebook and the service running training may also have different permissions.','A table belongs inside a schema and catalog. Reading it needs the relevant access at those boundaries, under the identity actually executing the job. Granting broad administrator access would conceal which permission was missing.'],prerequisites:[before('Run and data identities','databricks-mlflow-lineage'),before('Database query values and permissions','sql-parameters-permissions')],outputLabel:'Access decision and missing explicit requirements',checkpointTitle:'Which boundary is missing?',
  history:history("A Shared Table Needed a Shared Access Decision", [{"title":"Manage Unity Catalog privileges","url":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/manage-privileges/"},{"title":"Unity Catalog privilege reference","url":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control/privileges-reference"},{"title":"Unity Catalog history and open-source announcement, June 2024","url":"https://www.databricks.com/company/newsroom/press-releases/databricks-open-sources-unity-catalog-creating-industrys-only-open"}], "As teams used the same data from different workspaces and tools, finding a table and deciding who could read it became connected problems. Separate catalogs and access settings could disagree. A job that found a useful training table still needed an enforceable answer about whether its actual identity was allowed to use it.", "Databricks introduced Unity Catalog in 2021 as a shared governance system. The managed service now covers data and AI objects. Databricks announced an open-source Unity Catalog project in June 2024. The managed Databricks service and the open-source project are related products with different operational scope; the SQL reference here uses the managed service’s documented privileges.", "A catalog records what objects are called and where they belong. Access rules attach privileges to those objects and to principals, the users, groups or service identities receiving permission. We will follow one training job through those relationships, so a successful read in somebody else’s notebook cannot stand in for the job’s own permission check."),
  diagram:table('Read the complete object name','The same short table name can exist in another schema or catalog.',['Boundary','Object','Explicit privilege in this fixture'],[['Catalog','lab','USE CATALOG'],['Schema','lab.training','USE SCHEMA'],['Table','lab.training.features','SELECT']],'Each requirement must be satisfied for an authenticated principal or its established groups under this simplified explicit-grant model.'),
  parts:[
    p('Resolve the full name before asking who may read it',"Start by locating the object the job wants. In lab.training.features, lab names the catalog, training names a schema inside it, and features names the table. These names let the service distinguish this features table from a table with the same short name in another team’s schema.\n\nOur teaching check asks for permission to use the catalog and schema, then permission to read the table. Keep each privilege beside the object it refers to. A free-floating SELECT label would not tell us which table the job may read.",null,"A complete name makes the scope of the next permission question explicit."),
    p('Check the principal that actually runs the job','A principal is the identity receiving privileges, such as a user, group or service principal. A scheduled training job can run as a different principal from the notebook author. Inspect that execution context before editing grants.\n\nThe workshop receives verified principal and group identities as application-provided data. It does not let an agent authenticate itself by writing a group name. Real group membership must come from the identity system and be enforced at the data service.',null,'For a read under this explicit model, SELECT must be accompanied by USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA at the enclosing objects.'),
    withReference(p('Grant the needed boundary rather than hiding it','The SQL reference grants the explicit read permissions to an example training group. An authorised owner or administrator must choose and apply actual grants. Do not run it on a production catalog merely to make an exercise pass.\n\nReal Unity Catalog can inherit some privileges from ancestor objects, and owners or administrators can have other authority. The browser model checks direct explicit grants only. Use the platform’s actual effective permission evidence when diagnosing a real job.',null,'Permission to use a namespace is different from permission to read every table in it. Keep the selected table grant narrow when that matches the job’s scope.'),reference('Grant an isolated training group its declared table read','GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG lab TO `training-job-readers`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA lab.training TO `training-job-readers`;\nGRANT SELECT ON TABLE lab.training.features TO `training-job-readers`;\nSHOW GRANTS ON TABLE lab.training.features;',['Run only as an authorised grant manager in your own approved Unity Catalog environment. Replace the example group and object names deliberately.','These SQL statements change access. This is a reference to review, not a browser exercise that needs to be executed.'],'Inspect the effective principal and all relevant object levels. Do not assume a notebook author’s successful read proves a scheduled job has the same access.',refs.grants,'sql')),
    p('Preserve the missing requirements in a refusal','A useful refusal can say which declared boundary is missing without returning the protected rows. Our result is {allow, missing}; missing contains privilege/object pairs. Requiring every listed result prevents one successful SELECT check from masking a failed enclosing requirement.\n\nPassing this educational check does not authorise a real data request. The server and platform must still enforce current permission when data is accessed, including revocations after an earlier run was approved.',null,'Relevance, validation quality and permission answer different questions. A useful feature can still be forbidden to this training job.'),
    p('Test a different identity and a different schema','First provide SELECT but omit USE SCHEMA. Then keep all grant records unchanged and replace the principal with an unrelated job. Finally move the same short table name to another schema. Each case tests whether identity and object scope remain attached to the privilege.\n\nThe next lesson assumes the feature rows were obtained through a permitted read. It will ask which of those rows were available at the time of each prediction, another boundary permission alone cannot establish.',null,'The next decision is which historically available rows belong to each prediction.'),
  ],
  questions:[q("The only explicit grants for training-job are USE CATALOG on lab and SELECT on lab.training.features. This local check requires namespace usage as well as table read permission.",'What is missing?',["USE SCHEMA on lab.training","USE SCHEMA on lab.other","Another SELECT on a different table"],0,'The enclosing schema requirement remains unsatisfied.'),tf('A notebook author can read a table. A scheduled job runs as another service principal.','The author’s successful read proves the job has the same access.',false,'The effective executing identity can have different privileges.'),q('The allowed object is lab.training.features. A request now names lab.private.features.','Which scope changed?',['The schema and table object identity','Only a harmless display label','Only the row ordering'],0,'The short table name does not erase its containing namespace.'),tf('The workshop receives a list of trusted principal and group IDs.','Their authentication and group membership must be established outside this graph.',true,'Authentication and group establishment are explicit assumptions, not operations performed by this graph.'),q('All three explicit requirements pass, but a row contains a feature published tomorrow.',"Which remaining check would catch this particular problem?",["Historical feature availability","Repeat the same privilege checks without a time condition","Add another grant for the same table"],0,'Access permission does not establish temporal correctness.'),tf('Real Unity Catalog can include inherited privileges and administrative authority.','This direct-grant-only model omits mechanisms that can affect real effective permissions.',true,'The model deliberately excludes those additional mechanisms.')],
  practice:[challenge('Explain a table read refusal without granting an administrator role',["Write read_access(table, grants, principals). table is catalog.schema.table, with each name matching [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*; otherwise raise ValueError. grants is a list of dictionaries containing principal, object and privilege. principals is a trusted list of the actual identity and its established groups. Require direct matching grants for USE CATALOG on catalog, USE SCHEMA on catalog.schema, and SELECT on the full table. Return {\"allow\": boolean, \"missing\": [requirement strings]} in catalog/schema/table order. Do not infer inheritance or mutate inputs.","The new job reads operations.sensors.daily_features. One shown grant belongs to an analyst rather than the job. Print the job’s decision, then the number of the three required permissions it actually holds. This is an explicit-grant teaching check rather than a real authentication or Unity Catalog enforcement service."],"def read_access(table, grants, principals):\n    pass\n\ngrants = [{\"principal\":\"night-job\",\"object\":\"operations\",\"privilege\":\"USE CATALOG\"},{\"principal\":\"night-job\",\"object\":\"operations.sensors.daily_features\",\"privilege\":\"SELECT\"},{\"principal\":\"analyst\",\"object\":\"operations.sensors\",\"privilege\":\"USE SCHEMA\"}]\ndecision = read_access(\"operations.sensors.daily_features\", grants, [\"night-job\"])\nprint(decision)\nprint(3 - len(decision[\"missing\"]))","import re\n\ndef read_access(table, grants, principals):\n    pieces=table.split(\".\")\n    if len(pieces)!=3 or any(re.fullmatch(r\"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\",p) is None for p in pieces):\n        raise ValueError(\"Use a complete simple table name\")\n    requirements=[(\"USE CATALOG\",pieces[0]),(\"USE SCHEMA\",\".\".join(pieces[:2])),(\"SELECT\",table)]\n    missing=[privilege+\" \"+obj for privilege,obj in requirements\n             if not any(g[\"principal\"] in principals and g[\"object\"]==obj and g[\"privilege\"]==privilege for g in grants)]\n    return {\"allow\":not missing,\"missing\":missing}\n\ngrants = [{\"principal\":\"night-job\",\"object\":\"operations\",\"privilege\":\"USE CATALOG\"},{\"principal\":\"night-job\",\"object\":\"operations.sensors.daily_features\",\"privilege\":\"SELECT\"},{\"principal\":\"analyst\",\"object\":\"operations.sensors\",\"privilege\":\"USE SCHEMA\"}]\ndecision = read_access(\"operations.sensors.daily_features\", grants, [\"night-job\"])\nprint(decision)\nprint(3 - len(decision[\"missing\"]))","{'allow': False, 'missing': ['USE SCHEMA operations.sensors']}\n2",["First list the required privilege and object pairs for the complete table name. Then inspect grants belonging to the job or its supplied established groups.","A grant shown for analyst cannot help night-job unless that identity is actually in the trusted principal list. Return the missing requirements in catalog, schema, table order."],[["Complete group grant","all_grants=grants+[{\"principal\":\"team\",\"object\":\"operations.sensors\",\"privilege\":\"USE SCHEMA\"}]\nassert read_access(\"operations.sensors.daily_features\",all_grants,[\"night-job\",\"team\"]) == {\"allow\":True,\"missing\":[]}"],["Other principal","assert len(read_access(\"operations.sensors.daily_features\",grants,[\"other\"])[\"missing\"]) == 3"],["Scoped names","assert \"SELECT operations.other.daily_features\" in read_access(\"operations.other.daily_features\",grants,[\"night-job\"])[\"missing\"]"],["Reject ambiguous name","try: read_access(\"features\",grants,[\"night-job\"])\nexcept ValueError: pass\nelse: raise AssertionError(\"Need complete name\")"]], {"question":"Why does night-job still lack permission to read this table?"})],
  build:build('Check each boundary of the table read','The training job has SELECT, but one enclosing namespace permission is absent.','Derive the read requirements from the three-part name, check them against explicit grants for the verified identities, then return allow and missing.',{table:'Complete catalog.schema.table name',grants:'Explicit privilege records',principals:'Verified job identity and established groups'},[sample('SELECT without the schema boundary',{table:'lab.training.features',grants:[{principal:'job',object:'lab',privilege:'USE CATALOG'},{principal:'job',object:'lab.training.features',privilege:'SELECT'}],principals:['job']},{allow:false,missing:['USE SCHEMA lab.training']}),sample('The complete explicit read',{table:'lab.training.features',grants:[{principal:'team',object:'lab',privilege:'USE CATALOG'},{principal:'team',object:'lab.training',privilege:'USE SCHEMA'},{principal:'team',object:'lab.training.features',privilege:'SELECT'}],principals:['job','team']},{allow:true,missing:[]}),sample('Another job does not inherit its colleague’s identity',{table:'lab.training.features',grants:[{principal:'job-A',object:'lab',privilege:'USE CATALOG'},{principal:'job-A',object:'lab.training',privilege:'USE SCHEMA'},{principal:'job-A',object:'lab.training.features',privilege:'SELECT'}],principals:['job-B']},{allow:false,missing:['USE CATALOG lab','USE SCHEMA lab.training','SELECT lab.training.features']})],op('dbxAccessDecision',op('dbxCheckGrants',op('dbxReadRequirements',s('table')),s('grants'),s('principals'))),'A table read requires its declared object boundaries under the actual principal, not an unrelated successful grant.',"This local check considers direct explicit grants for the supplied verified identities. Authentication, group resolution and the platform’s full effective-permission rules remain the real service’s responsibility."),
  journey:journey('The run manifest names a valid table, but the scheduled training identity cannot read it.','Trace permission through the complete table name','List the catalog, schema and table requirements before comparing explicit grants for this job.','The refusal identifies a missing boundary without returning protected records.',diagnose('A colleague’s grant appears in the evidence','All three privileges belong to job-A; the current principal is job-B. Why must the construction refuse?', ['The privileges belong to a different identity','Matching object names do not transfer another principal’s authority.'],['The table must be corrupt','The shown evidence establishes an identity mismatch, not damaged data.'],'Keep principal, privilege and object attached while checking each boundary.'),[connection('databricks-mlflow-lineage','Name the exact source table','A manifest identifies what to read; it does not authorise the job to read it.'),connection('sql-parameters-permissions','Keep query values separate from database authority','Accepting a value in a query never grants the caller permission to read its source.')],'Where must this permission be enforced after a browser workshop says allow?','The authoritative data service must check the executing identity and current policy.',{path:[0,0],title:'Resolve the object boundaries',challenge:'Return the three explicit read requirements implied by the complete table name.',why:'Inspecting each object prevents a short table name from hiding another namespace.'},['SELECT on a table does not supply its enclosing usage requirements in this model.','Every required privilege needs a matching grant for this principal or an already established group.']),
  fresh:fresh('The short table name stays the same in another schema','The job has catalog usage and SELECT on lab.other.features, but schema usage is granted only on lab.training. Return the missing requirement.',{table:'lab.other.features',grants:[{principal:'job',object:'lab',privilege:'USE CATALOG'},{principal:'job',object:'lab.training',privilege:'USE SCHEMA'},{principal:'job',object:'lab.other.features',privilege:'SELECT'}],principals:['job']},{allow:false,missing:['USE SCHEMA lab.other']},'A privilege on the earlier schema does not satisfy the new object boundary.'),
});

const featureLookups = authored({
  id:'databricks-historical-features',title:'Give Each Prediction the Features It Could Actually Have Seen',topic:'Making feature access explicit',
  blurb:"The current temperature is useful for a prediction now. An old prediction needs the value that was available then, including a deliberate rule for missing or stale readings.",openingTitle:'The Training Table Knows the Answer Before Botie Could Have Known It',
  opening:['Botie can read the approved table. He now joins the latest temperature to every old inspection. Unfortunately, a temperature correction published after an inspection becomes an input to that inspection’s prediction. The training example has learned something from its own future.','We need a feature value belonging to that pump and available by that prediction time. We will also decide how old a feature may be. A missing value must remain visible rather than being quietly replaced by the newest reading.'],prerequisites:[before('Historical availability','data-asof-features'),before('Committed table versions','databricks-delta-snapshots'),before('Permitted feature access','databricks-unity-catalog-access')],outputLabel:'One historical feature result per prediction',checkpointTitle:'Which feature was available then?',
  history:history("Reusing a Feature Without Rewriting Its Past", [{"title":"Databricks point-in-time feature joins","url":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/feature-store/time-series"},{"title":"Feature tables and model training","url":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/feature-store/train-models-with-feature-store"},{"title":"Uber Engineering: Michelangelo and its shared feature store, September 2017","url":"https://www.uber.com/us/en/blog/michelangelo-machine-learning-platform/"}], "A feature is an input a model reads. Temperature is one simple feature in our example. When several models need the same input, rebuilding it separately can give training and live prediction different definitions. A feature store keeps reusable feature data and metadata so those consumers can request a shared input.", "Uber’s September 2017 account of its Michelangelo platform described a shared feature store for training and prediction. It is an influential published example of the need to reuse inputs consistently, rather than a claim that one company invented every part of feature storage. Databricks’ feature engineering interfaces address related work through reusable tables and model-linked lookups.", "Reusing a definition still leaves a time question. A prediction made before a correction was published could not have read that correction. Databricks documents point-in-time feature lookup for matching each prediction to its earlier eligible feature rows. Delta time travel chooses a table snapshot; this lookup chooses a row for each prediction within that retained history."),
  diagram:table('An event can be early while its usable feature is late','Times below are minutes from the same shift start.',['Feature','pump','publishedAt','value'],[['First usable value','P7','3','10'],['Later correction','P7','8','99'],['Another entity','P2','4','20']],'At minute 6, P7 can use the value published at minute 3. The minute-8 correction is unavailable even if its underlying event happened earlier.'),
  parts:[
    p('Write down the two kinds of rows',"Keep the two kinds of records visible. A feature-history row names a pump, the minute its value was published and the value. A prediction row names a pump and the minute when a decision needed to be made. These timestamps describe different events.\n\nFor a prediction about P7, begin with P7’s feature history. Then ask which values had been published by the decision minute. A recent reading from another pump cannot fill this input just because its timestamp is convenient.\n\nBoth Python inputs are lists of dictionaries. The same pump can occur in several prediction dictionaries because it needs decisions at different times. Our invented shift uses integer minutes from one start time; real timestamp fields need consistent types and a clear time-zone convention.",null,"Keep one result per prediction in its original order, including predictions with no usable feature."),
    p('Use availability, not a backdated event label','An event timestamp says when something happened. A publication timestamp says when this particular computed feature became usable. A correction can refer to an old event while arriving after the prediction.\n\nThe workshop compares publishedAt with the prediction’s at field. Published history must not be backdated or overwritten in a way that invents earlier availability. An as-of API cannot recover the true arrival history if the table falsely labels a later correction as previously known.',null,'Read the timestamp definition as an input contract: when did this computed value become usable?'),
    p('Select candidates before choosing the newest one','First match the pump. Then keep features published no later than the prediction. Finally apply the declared maximum age. The workshop treats both timestamp boundaries as inclusive. Only after those checks do we choose the greatest remaining publication time.\n\nIf no candidate remains, return null for that prediction’s feature. A legitimate missing value is different from zero, and neither is permission to read a future row. If the latest available row explicitly contains null, keep that null instead of quietly searching backward for a nicer value.', 'prediction minute = 6\nfeature publication minutes = [3, 8]\nmaximum age = 5 minutes\n3 <= 6 and 6 - 3 <= 5: eligible\n8 > 6: unavailable\nselected value = 10','A fresh sensor value can still be wrong; the earlier range and quality checks remain part of the pipeline.'),
    p('Keep snapshot identity separate from lookup time','A Delta snapshot identifies which committed contents of a table we read. A point-in-time lookup selects suitable feature rows inside those contents for each prediction timestamp. Both may be needed for an honest training reconstruction.\n\nRecord a feature-table snapshot and the lookup contract. If later backfills rewrite old feature rows, an unpinned latest-table read may change an old training set even though its prediction timestamps stay unchanged. Retained snapshots or immutable publication history prevent that change from being hidden.',null,'A feature key identifies the entity. The timestamp key must be explicitly designated for time-series lookup; merely adding a timestamp to an ordinary equality key does not create as-of semantics.'),
    withReference(p('Declare the time-series keys in the real feature interface','The optional reference registers a time-series feature table and builds a training set from prediction rows. FeatureLookup names the entity and prediction-timestamp columns. Its lookback window provides a training-time age bound.\n\nThe feature history is assumed to use actual publication availability as its timestamp. Registering a column cannot prove that semantic claim. Check the selected runtime’s table constraints and API support, and do not use preview pipeline primary-key features unless you deliberately choose a preview environment.',null,'Databricks distinguishes this historical join from online latest-value lookup. Do not assume a training lookback setting automatically enforces the same age policy in every online serving path.'),reference('Build a training set from published feature history','from datetime import timedelta\nfrom databricks.feature_engineering import FeatureEngineeringClient, FeatureLookup\n\nfe = FeatureEngineeringClient()\nfe.create_table(\n    name="lab.training.published_temperatures",\n    primary_keys=["pump", "published_at"],\n    timeseries_columns="published_at",\n    df=published_features,\n)\nlookup = FeatureLookup(\n    table_name="lab.training.published_temperatures",\n    feature_names=["temperature"], lookup_key="pump",\n    timestamp_lookup_key="prediction_at",\n    lookback_window=timedelta(minutes=5),\n)\ntraining_set = fe.create_training_set(\n    df=predictions, feature_lookups=[lookup], label="target",\n    exclude_columns=["pump", "prediction_at"],\n)\ntraining_rows = training_set.load_df()',['Use your own Unity Catalog-enabled Databricks workspace with a compatible databricks-feature-engineering client. Current Unity Catalog examples use timeseries_columns; timestamp_keys belongs to the legacy Workspace Feature Store interface.','Provide published_features with pump STRING, published_at TIMESTAMP and temperature DOUBLE, unique per entity/timestamp, and predictions with pump STRING, prediction_at TIMESTAMP and target. Use a consistent timestamp convention.','The reference creates a table; use a new approved name and inspect uniqueness, permission and retention requirements before running it. No workspace or credential is required for the local exercise.'],'The timestamp key activates historical lookup. It cannot make a backdated correction truthful or prove that an online serving policy uses the same age bound.',refs.features)),
    p('Make training and serving agree about missing and stale values','Training can return a missing feature for an early prediction. Serving needs an explicit policy for that same condition: use a declared fallback, request another measurement or decline the prediction. Filling missing values differently in the two paths creates a new model procedure.\n\nPublish and test the transformation version as well as feature values. A shared table name is insufficient if training calculates a five-minute window while serving uses an hour, or if one side clips readings and the other retains them.',null,'Pass missing results to a declared fallback policy after this lookup.'),
    p('Test the future, the boundary and the absent entity','Move only the prediction time across a publication boundary and predict when the selected value changes. Then shorten the maximum age so the previously eligible row becomes stale. Add another pump with a recent feature and make sure it never fills a missing value for the requested pump.\n\nConflicting values at the same entity and publication time are another data-contract failure. Stop rather than letting a distributed row order choose one. A fresh result should preserve which prediction is missing and why it could not use the later record.',null,'When a new correction arrives, inspect its publication timestamp before letting it change a historical training row.'),
  ],
  questions:[q('P7 features: publishedAt 3 has value 10; publishedAt 8 has value 99. Prediction P7 at 6, maximum age 5.','Which value can this lookup return?',['10','99','The average of 10 and 99'],0,'Only the value published by the prediction and within the age bound is eligible.'),tf('A correction arrives at minute 8 but describes an event from minute 2. The prediction happened at minute 6.','Using its old event time as if it were available then can leak future information.',true,'The correction’s arrival/publication time is later than the decision.'),q('P7 has no eligible feature. P2 has a fresh one at exactly the prediction time.','What belongs in P7’s feature result?',['null','P2’s value','The next future P7 value'],0,'A different entity or unavailable future cannot fill the missing input.'),tf('The latest available feature row explicitly has value null.','This lesson’s lookup should silently choose an older nonnull value instead.',false,'That would change the declared most-recent-value contract.'),q('A timestamp is included in ordinary equality keys but is not declared as a time-series column in the feature interface.',"Which matching rule needs an explicit time-series designation?",['Automatic as-of matching to an earlier timestamp','Equality matching on all supplied keys','That the timestamp column has a type'],0,'A time-series designation is needed for historical lookup semantics.'),tf('A run pins a retained Delta table version and also uses prediction timestamps for feature lookup.','These two choices can be necessary because they constrain different kinds of time.',true,'The snapshot constrains committed contents; the lookup constrains per-example temporal eligibility.'),q('A feature was published at minute 3, the prediction is minute 9, and maximum age is 5 under inclusive boundaries.','What should happen?',['The feature is too old and yields null if no other candidate exists','It stays eligible because it is the latest','The lookup should use the newest future row'],0,'The feature age is six minutes, exceeding the declared bound.'),tf('A real online serving path uses the latest feature value without enforcing the training lookback window.','A training-time lookback alone proves that online inputs obey the same age limit.',false,'The actual serving path must enforce its own declared freshness behavior.')],
  practice:[challenge('Reconstruct the feature available for each prediction',["Write historical_features(history, predictions, maximum_age). history contains dictionaries with pump (text), publishedAt (integer shift minute) and value (number or None). predictions contains pump and at in the same unit. Return one {\"pump\", \"at\", \"temperature\"} dictionary per prediction in original order. Match the pump, require publishedAt <= at and at - publishedAt <= maximum_age, then use the greatest eligible publishedAt. Return None when absent; preserve an explicit latest None. Conflicting values for one pump/publication timestamp raise ValueError. maximum_age is nonnegative. Do not mutate arguments.","The new history contains different feature values and three prediction rows. Print all three lookup results in their original order, then the sum of the numerical temperatures that were available. Keep None for the absent pump; it contributes nothing to that diagnostic sum. This is a local reconstruction under an immutable-publication contract."],"def historical_features(history, predictions, maximum_age):\n    pass\n\nhistory = [{\"pump\":\"P7\",\"publishedAt\":3,\"value\":14},{\"pump\":\"P7\",\"publishedAt\":8,\"value\":61}]\nresults = historical_features(history,[{\"pump\":\"P7\",\"at\":6},{\"pump\":\"P7\",\"at\":9},{\"pump\":\"P2\",\"at\":9}],5)\nprint(results)\nprint(sum(row[\"temperature\"] for row in results if row[\"temperature\"] is not None))","def historical_features(history, predictions, maximum_age):\n    versions={}\n    for row in history:\n        identity=(row[\"pump\"],row[\"publishedAt\"])\n        if identity in versions and versions[identity] != row[\"value\"]:\n            raise ValueError(\"Conflicting feature version\")\n        versions[identity]=row[\"value\"]\n    result=[]\n    for prediction in predictions:\n        pump, at=prediction[\"pump\"],prediction[\"at\"]\n        candidates=[r for r in history if r[\"pump\"]==pump and r[\"publishedAt\"]<=at and at-r[\"publishedAt\"]<=maximum_age]\n        latest=max(candidates,key=lambda r:r[\"publishedAt\"]) if candidates else None\n        result.append({\"pump\":pump,\"at\":at,\"temperature\":latest[\"value\"] if latest else None})\n    return result\n\nhistory = [{\"pump\":\"P7\",\"publishedAt\":3,\"value\":14},{\"pump\":\"P7\",\"publishedAt\":8,\"value\":61}]\nresults = historical_features(history,[{\"pump\":\"P7\",\"at\":6},{\"pump\":\"P7\",\"at\":9},{\"pump\":\"P2\",\"at\":9}],5)\nprint(results)\nprint(sum(row[\"temperature\"] for row in results if row[\"temperature\"] is not None))","[{'pump': 'P7', 'at': 6, 'temperature': 14}, {'pump': 'P7', 'at': 9, 'temperature': 61}, {'pump': 'P2', 'at': 9, 'temperature': None}]\n75",["Keep each prediction beside its own candidates. Match the pump, exclude later publication times and apply the inclusive age limit before choosing the newest.","If that newest eligible row explicitly contains None, keep None. Searching backward for a nicer value would change the declared lookup rule."],[["Boundary and stale value","assert historical_features(history,[{\"pump\":\"P7\",\"at\":8}],0)[0][\"temperature\"] == 61\nassert historical_features(history,[{\"pump\":\"P7\",\"at\":7}],3)[0][\"temperature\"] is None"],["Different entity and preserved order","r=historical_features(history,[{\"pump\":\"missing\",\"at\":8},{\"pump\":\"P7\",\"at\":6}],5)\nassert [x[\"temperature\"] for x in r] == [None,14]"],["Explicit latest null","h=[{\"pump\":\"A\",\"publishedAt\":1,\"value\":5},{\"pump\":\"A\",\"publishedAt\":2,\"value\":None}]\nassert historical_features(h,[{\"pump\":\"A\",\"at\":3}],5)[0][\"temperature\"] is None"],["Conflicting feature version","try: historical_features(history+[dict(history[0],value=4)],[],5)\nexcept ValueError: pass\nelse: raise AssertionError(\"Expected conflict\")"],["No mutation","before=repr(history)\nhistorical_features(history,[{\"pump\":\"P7\",\"at\":6}],5)\nassert repr(history) == before"]], {"question":"Which reading could each prediction have used at the time?"})],
  build:build('Keep every prediction on its own feature history','The current table is permitted, but a later correction leaks into earlier training rows.','For every prediction, form its same-entity candidates within the publication and age bounds, then choose the latest eligible feature or null. Preserve prediction order.',{history:'Immutable published feature history',predictions:'Entity and decision minute per prediction',maximumAge:'Maximum feature age in minutes'},[sample('A later correction is unavailable',{history:[{pump:'P7',publishedAt:3,value:10},{pump:'P7',publishedAt:8,value:99},{pump:'P2',publishedAt:4,value:20}],predictions:[{pump:'P7',at:6},{pump:'P2',at:6}],maximumAge:5},[{pump:'P7',at:6,temperature:10},{pump:'P2',at:6,temperature:20}]),sample('The old value becomes stale before the correction',{history:[{pump:'P7',publishedAt:3,value:10},{pump:'P7',publishedAt:8,value:99}],predictions:[{pump:'P7',at:7}],maximumAge:3},[{pump:'P7',at:7,temperature:null}]),sample('A latest missing value is still the latest row',{history:[{pump:'P7',publishedAt:3,value:10},{pump:'P7',publishedAt:5,value:null}],predictions:[{pump:'P7',at:6},{pump:'other',at:6}],maximumAge:5},[{pump:'P7',at:6,temperature:null},{pump:'other',at:6,temperature:null}])],op('dbxLatestFeature',op('dbxHistoricalCandidates',s('history'),s('predictions'),s('maximumAge'))),'Entity, availability and age eligibility are established before newest-value selection.',"The floor looks up supplied immutable publication history using an inclusive age rule. Computing the features, establishing truthful availability timestamps and operating a real feature service are separate responsibilities."),
  journey:journey('Access is correct, but the latest-value join gives past predictions information published later.','Build the feature candidates for each decision','Keep each prediction’s entity and time beside its candidates, then select the newest eligible published value.','Future, stale and wrong-entity values cannot silently fill a historical model input.',diagnose('The newest value was published after the prediction','P7 at minute 6 sees values published at 3 and 8. A broken join chooses the minute-8 correction because its event was earlier. What should be repaired?',['The availability boundary before latest-value selection','A correction’s old event label does not make its newly published value available earlier.'],['The prediction’s timestamp should move forward until the result looks good','Changing the decision time rewrites the prediction problem.'],'Predict the eligible candidate list before choosing its newest row.'),[connection('data-asof-features','Respect what was available at the decision','The platform lookup must preserve that same historical boundary.'),connection('databricks-delta-snapshots','Read an identified committed input','A timestamp filter cannot recover overwritten historical contents.')],'What evidence shows that publishedAt really records availability rather than a backdated event?', 'Keep publication history, table identity and the lookup/fallback policy with the trained procedure.',{path:[0],title:'Keep eligible candidates beside each prediction',challenge:'Return one candidate group per prediction after entity, time and age filtering.',why:'An empty candidate group should remain visible rather than being replaced by another pump’s recent value.'},['Match the entity first and exclude later publication times before selecting a maximum.','A latest explicit null is not permission to search backward for a nonnull value.']),
  fresh:fresh('One pump is stale while another has just published','At minute 8, A’s minute-5 value is too old for a two-minute limit and its next value arrives at 9. B has a value at 8. Preserve both prediction rows.',{history:[{pump:'A',publishedAt:5,value:1},{pump:'A',publishedAt:9,value:7},{pump:'B',publishedAt:8,value:4}],predictions:[{pump:'A',at:8},{pump:'B',at:8}],maximumAge:2},[{pump:'A',at:8,temperature:null},{pump:'B',at:8,temperature:4}],'Missingness belongs to the particular prediction; a different entity’s fresh value cannot repair it.'),
});

// Small runnable components teach the data structure before the fuller challenge.
shuffle.parts[1].examples = [example('Count the original keys before changing their routes', 'A dictionary maps each original pump identifier to its accumulated count. The routing marker does not belong in this logical count.', 'counts = {}\nfor pump in ["A", "A", "B"]:\n    counts[pump] = counts.get(pump, 0) + 1\nprint(counts)', "{'A': 2, 'B': 1}", 'Keep this original-key result as a correctness reference when later experimenting with worker routes.')];
snapshots.parts[0].examples = [example('Inspect the nesting of a retained snapshot', 'The outer list holds table versions. Inside one matching dictionary, rows holds the complete sensor records.', 'history = [{"version":0,"rows":[{"pump":"A","temperature":10}]}, {"version":1,"rows":[{"pump":"A","temperature":30}]}]\nmatching = [entry for entry in history if entry["version"] == 0]\nprint(matching[0]["rows"])', "[{'pump': 'A', 'temperature': 10}]", 'The challenge must additionally reject absent or duplicated version identities and handle an empty committed snapshot.')];
ingestion.parts[0].examples = [example('Ask whether a file identity has completed', 'A set supports membership checks without attaching a numerical meaning to an identifier.', 'completed = {"f0", "f2"}\nprint("f0" not in completed)\nprint("f1" not in completed)', 'False\nTrue', 'This checks only committed identities. The full ingestion procedure also has to suppress repeated new discoveries and return progress with the output.')];
changes.parts[2].examples = [example('Compare the source sequence before replacing state', 'A deleted record still has an entity identity and source sequence. Reading those fields does not require a numerical value to be present.', 'stored = {"pump":"A","seq":9,"deleted":True,"value":None}\nincoming = {"pump":"A","seq":8,"deleted":False,"value":22}\nprint(incoming["seq"] > stored["seq"])', 'False', 'The arriving active value is older. The fuller challenge handles batches, independent entities and contradictory ties.')];
quality.parts[0].examples = [example('Keep each condition result beside its record ID', 'The boolean marks the condition only. It does not decide which rows should be published.', 'records = [{"id":"r1","temperature":10},{"id":"r2","temperature":None}]\nchecks = [(row["id"], row["temperature"] is not None and row["temperature"] >= 0) for row in records]\nprint(checks)', "[('r1', True), ('r2', False)]", 'Warn, drop and fail consume this evidence differently. The later challenge implements those consequences.')];
tracking.parts[1].examples = [example("A table-local version needs its table identity", "Both rows have version four and the same split. Keep the row from the declared assessment table.", "runs = [{\"runId\":\"a\",\"validationTable\":\"lab.validation.easy_readings\",\"validationVersion\":4,\"split\":\"S\"}, {\"runId\":\"b\",\"validationTable\":\"lab.validation.sensor_readings\",\"validationVersion\":4,\"split\":\"S\"}]\nprint([row[\"runId\"] for row in runs if row[\"validationTable\"] == \"lab.validation.sensor_readings\" and row[\"validationVersion\"] == 4 and row[\"split\"] == \"S\"])", "['b']", "The full selector also checks assessment role, ranks the eligible errors and preserves both input table identities in the manifest.")];
governance.parts[0].examples = [example('A privilege is paired with its object', 'Tuples can keep privilege and object together in a set. Matching SELECT does not make the enclosing requirements disappear.', 'required = {("USE CATALOG","lab"),("USE SCHEMA","lab.training"),("SELECT","lab.training.features")}\ngranted = {("SELECT","lab.training.features")}\nprint(sorted(required - granted))', "[('USE CATALOG', 'lab'), ('USE SCHEMA', 'lab.training')]", 'This component omits principal identity. The challenge adds that boundary instead of assuming every shown grant belongs to the caller.')];
featureLookups.parts[2].examples = [example('Find a prediction’s available same-pump candidates', 'Start with entity and availability. This small component has not yet applied the age limit or chosen the newest row.', 'history = [{"pump":"P7","publishedAt":3,"value":10},{"pump":"P7","publishedAt":8,"value":99},{"pump":"P2","publishedAt":4,"value":20}]\ncandidates = [row for row in history if row["pump"] == "P7" and row["publishedAt"] <= 6]\nprint([(row["publishedAt"], row["value"]) for row in candidates])', '[(3, 10)]', 'The complete lookup additionally applies freshness, preserves every prediction row and handles absent or explicitly missing latest values.')];

// Begin before a correction exists, then test the same construction after it
// arrives. A loader that follows latest now changes an old training result.
snapshots.build.cases.unshift(sample('Before the later correction exists', {
  history: [{version:0,rows:[{pump:'P7',temperature:10},{pump:'P2',temperature:20}]}],
  version:0,field:'temperature',
},15));
featureLookups.build.cases.unshift(sample('Only the available feature rows have arrived', {
  history:[{pump:'P7',publishedAt:3,value:10},{pump:'P2',publishedAt:4,value:20}],
  predictions:[{pump:'P7',at:6},{pump:'P2',at:6}],maximumAge:5,
},[{pump:'P7',at:6,temperature:10},{pump:'P2',at:6,temperature:20}]));
// Investigations use the changed case, after the initial construction example.
snapshots.journey.investigation.caseIndex = 1;
governance.journey.investigation.caseIndex = 2;
featureLookups.journey.investigation.caseIndex = 1;

const readingFragment = title => title.toLowerCase().replace(/['’]/g, '').replace(/&/g, 'and').replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-').replace(/^-|-$/g, '');
const originalLazyParts = lazy.parts;
const originalLazyTechnical = originalLazyParts.find(part => part.title === lazy.technicalStart);
const originalLazyCheckpoint = originalLazyParts.find(part => part.title === lazy.checkpoints[0].after);
const originalLazyCheckpointId = readingFragment(lazy.checkpoints[0].title);
lazy.quizId = `questions-on-parts-1-to-${originalLazyParts.length}`;
const platformFoundations = [
  p('Why would one cleaning rule need several computers?',
    'One file of pump readings is easy to inspect with a Python program. Years of readings from many sites are a different workload. The files may exceed one machine’s memory, or the team may need an answer sooner than one process can produce it. We can divide work into portions and give several processes the same declared operation.\n\nApache Spark is an engine for organising and executing that kind of calculation. A portion of the records is called a partition. For a filter, separate partitions can apply the same condition independently; a later grouped total may have to combine their contributions. Spark can also run locally for small work, so its name does not by itself mean that several machines are involved.\n\nA driver coordinates the application. Executor processes carry out tasks on the available compute. Describing operations through Spark’s table interface lets the engine choose how to carry them out while preserving the requested result.',
    null,
    'Adding workers changes where the calculation runs. We still have to explain which records the calculation should return.'),
  p('Who keeps that shared work running?',
    'A working filter leaves the team with practical questions. Where will the scheduled job run? How will a colleague inspect the code and the records from a failed attempt? Providing a shared environment is work of its own.\n\nDatabricks is a managed cloud platform for this work. Its workspace gives a team a place to develop and run data and machine-learning jobs while the service manages supported compute and connects the work to configured storage and access controls. In these lessons we use Spark there for the table calculations. Apache Spark also exists independently as an open-source project.\n\nChoosing the platform does not choose a cleaning rule for us. We still decide what an invalid sensor value means and what a model may learn from it. Later lessons will follow the other responsibilities that appear once the calculation is shared: keeping a table version, recording a training attempt and checking the job’s permission to read an input.',
    null,
    'The local workshops let us inspect those decisions on a handful of records. The optional code references show where the same ideas meet a real Databricks workspace.'),
];
for (const [index, part] of originalLazyParts.entries()) {
  part.id ??= readingFragment(part.title);
  part.title = `${index + platformFoundations.length + 1}. ${part.title.replace(/^\d+\.\s*/, '')}`;
}
lazy.parts = [
  ...platformFoundations.map((part,index) => ({...part,title:`${index+1}. ${part.title}`})),
  ...originalLazyParts,
];
lazy.technicalStart = originalLazyTechnical.title;
lazy.checkpoints[0].after = originalLazyCheckpoint.title;
lazy.checkpoints[0].id = originalLazyCheckpointId;
lazy.checkpoints[0].title = `Questions on Parts 1 to ${lazy.parts.indexOf(originalLazyCheckpoint)+1}`;
lazy.checkpoints[0].questions.push(tf(
  'A team moves a Spark calculation from a managed Databricks workspace to machines it manages itself.',
  'The team can continue using Spark as its computation engine.',
  true,
  'Spark is an independent open-source engine. Databricks supplies a managed environment in which the team can use it.',
));
lazy.reading.connectionTitle = platformFoundations[0].title;

export const databricksRecords = [lazy, shuffle, snapshots, ingestion, changes, quality, tracking, governance, featureLookups];
export const databricksJourneys = Object.fromEntries(databricksRecords.map(record => [record.id, record.journey]));
export const databricksFreshCases = Object.fromEntries(databricksRecords.map(record => [record.id, record.fresh]));
const lessons = databricksRecords.map(record => {
  const result = { ...record };
  for (const field of ['journey', 'fresh', 'outputLabel', 'checkpointTitle']) delete result[field];
  return result;
});
export default lessons;
